{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-9388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/2edd37e6318a7d78b3e6f496bb3f688d006949ccab983f75cece9993817f6b69.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-9388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-9388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c723b8b88ccecb0d33f26c603d5feb105dfe00d92f9224028f04d5698bf288b4"} {"question" "q-9388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-9388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta "] ["math" "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta "] ["math" "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-9388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-142436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/c7fd7d815bd006c27be4fc793e4cdc8c66ccd119d86167ad7f7cb1da64a4f2bd.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-142436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta , \\\\ y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-142436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c723b8b88ccecb0d33f26c603d5feb105dfe00d92f9224028f04d5698bf288b4"} {"question" "q-142436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-142436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡3\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡3\\theta "] ["math" "{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-142436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-142437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/5bbb314322763dad82b0390e9d9713ab551de16208a2eb0b75c4ceb3c1f9c611.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-142437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(-5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-142437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c723b8b88ccecb0d33f26c603d5feb105dfe00d92f9224028f04d5698bf288b4"} {"question" "q-142437" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-142437" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta "] ["math" "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "-{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta "] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-142437" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-141051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/6c07657992f5c4f708cd7546902c5e26206bfa41f291a68ec7b582cb18a167fe.png)\n\nConsider the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,$ shown above. Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a-b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-141051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ \n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2)+2(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+(-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=-2,$ and so \n\n$$\na-b=2-(-2)=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-141051" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c723b8b88ccecb0d33f26c603d5feb105dfe00d92f9224028f04d5698bf288b4"} {"question" "q-141051" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-141051" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-141051" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-352489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/50ed8b3093adf431f41e6332fa43ac122df79c98d487961037443c77b4290fb7.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=6-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above, calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$ Fill in the missing parts in the result below.\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-352489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=6-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(6-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =(6-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =6{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′}-{({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =6(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′} \\\\  & =6{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-352489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c723b8b88ccecb0d33f26c603d5feb105dfe00d92f9224028f04d5698bf288b4"} {"question" "q-352489" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-352489" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-352489" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-352489" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-149913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for the polar curve $r=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-149913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ))}{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-149913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c723b8b88ccecb0d33f26c603d5feb105dfe00d92f9224028f04d5698bf288b4"} {"question" "q-149913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-149913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )"] ["math" "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta )"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2\\theta )"] ["math" "-\\sqrt{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta )"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-149913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-352518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polar curve $r=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ as a function of $\\theta $ given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-352518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ))}{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-352518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c723b8b88ccecb0d33f26c603d5feb105dfe00d92f9224028f04d5698bf288b4"} {"question" "q-352518" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-352518" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{5}\\sec(2\\theta)^3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-348139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ calculate $\\frac{{d}^{2}y}{d{x}^{2}}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n$\\frac{{d}^{2}y}{d{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-348139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{d}^{2}y}{d{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,y=3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ))}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-348139" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c723b8b88ccecb0d33f26c603d5feb105dfe00d92f9224028f04d5698bf288b4"} {"question" "q-348139" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-348139" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}\\sec(2\\theta)^3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-75295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for the polar curve $r=2,$ given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-75295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ))}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-75295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c723b8b88ccecb0d33f26c603d5feb105dfe00d92f9224028f04d5698bf288b4"} {"question" "q-75295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-75295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )"] ["math" "2{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )"] ["math" "3{csc}^{2}⁡(\\theta )"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-75295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-149909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for the polar curve $r=-\\sqrt{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-149909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=-\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\nx=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-149909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c723b8b88ccecb0d33f26c603d5feb105dfe00d92f9224028f04d5698bf288b4"} {"question" "q-149909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-149909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta "] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "q-149909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/state.json" "file_sha256" "552b9f4218280cc3d38160c6d2954856a65f453c3b47a54d74f4fbf3223ac37e"} {"question" "e-10033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/bdeef265fec0703b42b8d04e55502da49e8e84624a761368edd1dd5c106c7dbc.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r =-2 \\cos\\theta,$ shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/example-10033.json" "file_sha256" "a794e857c43ac53a63f5d65f9a9e396fe79942eda937d2c550fb03169666e66d"} {"question" "e-10033" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=-2\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta.$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/example-10033.json" "file_sha256" "a794e857c43ac53a63f5d65f9a9e396fe79942eda937d2c550fb03169666e66d"} {"question" "e-631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ for the polar curve $r=5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/example-631.json" "file_sha256" "0c140a097007071de0c9680a21cbf679a9b5891924d8b0f25e35e4a6bf2d6b2e"} {"question" "e-631" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta.$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=5,$ we get\n\n$$\nx =5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = -5\\sin\\theta, \\qquad y'(\\theta) = 5\\cos\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{ 5\\cos\\theta }{  -5\\sin\\theta} =-\\cot\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/example-631.json" "file_sha256" "0c140a097007071de0c9680a21cbf679a9b5891924d8b0f25e35e4a6bf2d6b2e"}] "usage" {"e-10033" [] "e-631" [] "q-9388" []} "basis" 2218 "retractions" [[17592186188903 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3854/Source/Images/q-9388.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for the polar curve $r = \\cos θ$, shown above."] [17592186187910 :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r =-2 \\cos\\theta$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3854/Source/Images/dc31fd8c1852c7552760d954f4a6b91b.png)"] [17592186187910 :question/worked-solution "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nr=-2\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -2\\cos θ\\cos θ =-2\\cos^{2} θ, \\\\\ny &= -2\\cos θ\\sin θ =-\\sin 2θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)$.\n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$:\n$x^{′}(θ)|= (-2\\cos^{2} θ)^{′}; =-2 \\cdot 2\\cos θ \\cdot (-\\sin θ); = 4\\sin θ\\cos θ; = 2\\sin 2θ$\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$:\n$y^{′}(θ)|= (-\\sin 2θ)^{′}; = - (\\cos 2θ \\cdot 2); =-2\\cos 2θ$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2\\cos 2θ}{2\\sin 2θ} \\\\\n&=-\\cot 2θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188128 :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ for the polar curve $r=5$."] [17592186188128 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} = (\\frac{d}{dθ}(\\frac{dy}{dx}))/((\\frac{dx}{dθ}))\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta$.\n\nUsing the fact that $r=5$, we get\n\n$$\nx =5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nThen, to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the rule\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(θ) &= -5\\sin θ, \\\\\ny^{′}(θ) &= 5\\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{5\\cos θ}{-5\\sin θ} \\\\\n&=-\\cot θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= (\\frac{d}{dθ}(-\\cot θ))/(-5\\sin θ) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\csc^{2} θ}{\\sin θ} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\csc^{2} θ \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sin θ} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\csc^{2} θ \\cdot \\csc θ \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{5}\\csc^{3} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186187899 17592186187900 17592186187901 17592186187902 17592186187903] "immutable_fields" [[17592186187898 :answer-field/correct] [17592186187898 :answer-field/id] [17592186187898 :answer-field/key] [17592186187898 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
